c"><p>That's a very problematic question. It's not really clear how to comp=
are between words with same frequency.<br>
In one example the words "i" and "love" have the same frequency, and "i" ap=
pears in the solution first. This means that if we use a min-heap, we could=
 break a tie by saying "i" is larger because it appears first lexicographic=
ally.<br>
In another example "a" and "aaa" both have the same frequency, but only "aa=
a" appears in the solution due to the bound of k. This means that if we use=
 a min-heap, then breaking the tie of the frequencies, "aaa" should be cons=
idered larger than "a", which makes "a" being popped out when "aaa" is inse=
rted... But this time "aaa" is the word which appears lexicographically aft=
er "a"!!<br>
These two examples show that we cannot just use a basic min-heap to compare=
 words first by their frequency count and then by their lexicographic order=
. Or we could do it, but then we need a larger heap than size k, in order t=
o break ties of frequency by ourselves at the end and not rely on the simpl=
e heap mechanism. If we do hold a larger than k sized heap, or if we use so=
me more mechanisms, then it's actually not really clear how to satisfy the =
follow-up at the end: to have an upper time complexity bound of O(n logk)</=
p>
<p>Very annoying question, which should have been phrased differently!</p><=
